 ## Overview
-Canonical Face Space Transformation normalizes world coordinates to a canonical face space using a rigid transform to address face shape variations between training data and real-world inputs, making the NeRF robust to geometry and pose variations [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from methods that rely on detailed mesh priors or non-rigid deformation fields by using only a rigid alignment derived from a mean geometry, avoiding the need for mesh details during view synthesis [S1, unofficial].
+Canonical Face Space Transformation normalizes world coordinates to a canonical face space using a rigid transform to address face shape variations between training data and real-world inputs, making the NeRF robust to geometry and pose variations [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the challenge of rendering novel views of faces from a single frontal image by normalizing geometric variations across different subjects into a shared canonical space, allowing a single pre-trained NeRF model to be fine-tuned for individual subjects [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from methods that rely on detailed mesh priors or non-rigid deformation fields by using only a rigid alignment derived from a mean geometry, avoiding the need for mesh details during view synthesis [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A point $x$ in the subject’s world coordinate system; viewing direction $\tilde{d}$ in world coordinates.
-*   **Output:** A point $x'$ in the face canonical space; color and occlusion values retrieved from the MLP $f_\theta$.
-*   **Parameters:** Optimized scale $s_m$, rotation $R_m$, and translation $t_m$ for subject $m$.
+*   **Input:** A point $x$ in the subject’s world coordinate system; viewing direction $\tilde{d}$ in world coordinates [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Output:** A point $x_0$ in the face canonical space; color and occlusion values retrieved from the MLP $f_\theta$ [S1, unofficial].
